The wasm backend has no encoding for alsa-lib's two module-level inline-asm
idioms (same wasm-can't-do-native-asm class as libffi/fpcast/__unmapself in
CLAUDE.md); both fire even with --without-versioned:

1. include/alsa-symbols.h: the no-versioned-symbols fallback of
   use_default_symbol_version aliases the public symbol name to the internal
   __-prefixed definition with ".weak name" + ".set name,real" asm — every TU
   using the macro (control.c, pcm.c, timer*.c, ump.c, dlmisc.c) fails with
   "<inline asm>: unknown directive". Express the exact same alias at the C
   level instead: a weak alias attribute, which clang lowers to a proper weak
   wasm symbol.

2. include/local.h: link_warning() (deprecation warnings via the ELF
   .gnu.warning.SYMBOL section) is gated on HAVE_GNU_LD/HAVE_ELF, which the
   header HARDCODES — its ".section …\n\t.previous" asm has no wasm encoding
   either. Route wasm to the header's own no-op fallback ("We will never be
   heard; they will all die horribly.") — losing only a link-time deprecation
   warning, no functional change.

Wasm-only (#if defined(__wasm__)); every other platform keeps the asm paths
bit-for-bit.

--- a/include/alsa-symbols.h
+++ b/include/alsa-symbols.h
@@ -54,7 +54,10 @@
 		default_symbol_version(real, name, version)
 #else
 #define use_symbol_version(real, name, version) /* nothing */
-#if defined(__alpha__) || defined(__mips__)
+#if defined(__wasm__)
+#define use_default_symbol_version(real, name, version) \
+	extern __typeof(real) name __attribute__((weak, alias(#real)))
+#elif defined(__alpha__) || defined(__mips__)
 #define use_default_symbol_version(real, name, version) \
 	__asm__ (".weak " ASM_NAME(#name)); \
 	__asm__ (ASM_NAME(#name) " = " ASM_NAME(#real))
--- a/include/local.h
+++ b/include/local.h
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@
 
 #define ASM_NAME(name) __SYMBOL_PREFIX name
 
-#ifdef HAVE_GNU_LD
+#if defined(HAVE_GNU_LD) && !defined(__wasm__)
 # ifdef HAVE_ELF
 
 /* We want the .gnu.warning.SYMBOL section to be unallocated.  */
